    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d864">

                <pb facs="1162" n="1148" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg1148"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.42793/8–2444: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-08-24T12:00:00+07:00">August 24,
                            1944—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 1:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1450. High official of Ministry of Education states privately that ban on private
                    students to the United States soon to be lifted; that 1200 students would
                    proceed to the United States in autumn after further local training; that
                    maximum of 30% of these would be members of Kuomintang or San Min Chu I Youth
                    Corps; and that no efforts would be made to control [thought?] while in United
                    States (Embassy’s No. 1371, August 9, 1 p.m.<anchor n="44" xml:id="d864-f1"/>)
                    Official made no mention of how transportation is to be arranged. Acute surface
                    transportation problem from India apparently unchanged.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
